From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 12:52:46 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Size SVG images from their CSS natural dimensions
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

CSS natural dimensions need three inputs the old shape couldn't give:
the absolute root attrs (with zero as a real dimension and negatives/
percentages as absent; the old is_absolute_length accepted -5), the
viewBox aspect ratio for deriving a missing axis, and a parsed tree even
when usvg rejects the root size as invalid. Dimensions now parse from
the attributes directly, view_box_aspect rides SvgImageData, and an
InvalidSize retry strips the offending root attrs so zero/negative sized
documents still build (they previously failed outright).

Layout then uses them:

- parse_svg_image normalizes the usvg viewport to the CSS natural
  dimensions: usvg resolves a missing axis against the viewBox edge
  (width=100 + viewBox 200x200 gave a 100x200 tree) and the painted
  tree is scaled to the layout box, so any tree/naturals mismatch
  letterboxed the content. Derived axes (ratio or 300/150 default)
  and the dimensionless 300x150 case are stamped onto the root.
- The measure path computes SVG inherent size from the naturals;
  ratio-only documents carry natural_ratio so block-level boxes fill
  the available inline size (CSS2 §10.3.2) while inline-level ones
  contain-fit 300x150, and dimensionless documents read the default
  object size with no ratio coupling between axes.

svg_image_data() exposes the full SvgImageData: the painter needs
viewBox presence (view_box_aspect) to tell a real author viewBox from
usvg's synthesized natural size, and svg_data() only surfaced the tree.

Normalization reads CSS width/height off the root through the intrinsic
metadata, so it sees the same dimensions as usvg.
